            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-xl",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>A humidifier that is too small for the room runs constantly and still cannot keep up. One that is too large creates condensation on windows and humidity levels that encourage mold. Getting the size right makes all the difference, and the process is simpler than most people think.</p><p>Here is how to do it in seven steps.</p><h3><strong>How to Choose the Right Humidifier</strong></h3>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "s3"
          }
        },
        "image_caption_QBGK76": {
          "type": "image_caption",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-xxl",
            "max_width_desktop": "1096px",
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/image2_9741d1a9-01c2-4961-b71e-af55acbd3b06.jpg",
            "image_desktop": "shopify://shop_images/image2_9741d1a9-01c2-4961-b71e-af55acbd3b06.jpg",
            "caption": "",
            "caption_style": "p6",
            "product": ""
          }
        },
        "text_e9yarP": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-lg",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-lg",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<h4><strong>Step 1: Measure the Room</strong></h4><p>Start with the square footage of the room where the humidifier will be used. Length times width. A rough estimate is fine, no need to measure down to the inch.</p><p>A<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/blogs/news/blog-small-rooms\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">small bedroom</span></a> or home office under 300 sq ft falls into the small category. A larger bedroom, nursery, or family room between 300 and 500 sq ft is considered medium. An open-plan living area or studio between 500 and 1,000 sq ft is large. Anything above 1,000 sq ft requires a whole-home solution or multiple devices.</p><h4><strong>Step 2: Match Coverage to Product Rating</strong></h4><p>Humidifiers list a maximum coverage area in square footage. The unit chosen should be rated to cover at least the room's actual size. A unit rated for 300 sq ft placed in a 500 sq ft room will struggle and run nonstop. A unit rated for 1,000 sq ft in a 200 sq ft bedroom will over-humidify the space.</p><p>A good rule is to go slightly above the room's square footage, not below. A 350 sq ft rated unit for a 280 sq ft bedroom is a comfortable match.</p><h4><strong>Step 3: Factor in Home Conditions</strong></h4><p>Square footage is only the starting point. Several other factors change what a space actually needs.</p><p><strong>Ceiling height</strong> matters because most coverage ratings assume 8-foot ceilings. Vaulted or 10-foot ceilings increase the air volume being humidified. Adding roughly 10 to 15% to the size estimate accounts for taller rooms.</p><p><strong>Climate and season</strong> play a role, too. In very cold, dry winters, significant moisture is lost through walls and ventilation. A slightly more powerful unit keeps up better during those peak-demand months.</p><p><strong>Drafts and air leaks</strong> are worth considering. Older homes with poor insulation lose humidity faster. If the home is drafty, sizing up is the safer choice.</p><h4><strong>Step 4: Choose the Right Type for the Room</strong></h4><p>For<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/blogs/news/blog-bedrooms\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">bedrooms</span></a>, ultrasonic cool mist is the best choice. Nearly silent, safe around kids and pets, and energy-efficient. For anyone who also wants air purification in the same space, the<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/products/2-in-1-pro-purify-humidify\"><span style=\"text-decoration:underline\"> Blueair 2-in-1 Pro Purify + Humidify</span></a> covers rooms up to 391 ft² with both functions in one unit using 360-degree InvisibleMist humidification and HEPASilent™ air purification.</p><p>For small rooms and<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/blogs/news/baby\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">nurseries</span></a>, a compact cool mist humidifier under 300 sq ft capacity is sufficient. The smaller tank means more frequent refills, but the footprint fits better on a nightstand.</p><p>For a<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/products/2-in-1-pro-purify-humidify\"><span style=\"text-decoration:underline\"> large living room</span></a> or open plan, an evaporative or ultrasonic unit rated for at least 500 sq ft is necessary. Large rooms tend to have more airflow, which dries things out faster.</p><p>For the whole house, whole-home humidifiers connect directly to the HVAC system and humidify every room simultaneously. More expensive upfront, but maintenance is lower, and coverage is consistent.</p><h4><strong>Step 5: Look at Tank Size and Daily Output</strong></h4><p>Tank size determines how often refills are needed. Most bedroom humidifiers have tanks between 0.5 and 1.5 gallons. Larger units for living areas go up to 3 to 4 gallons.</p><p>More practically, moisture output in gallons per day is the number that matters most. A unit producing 1 gallon per day can maintain comfortable humidity in most average-sized bedrooms without running dry overnight. For larger spaces, aim for 2 or more gallons per day.</p><p>The<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/products/2-in-1-pro-purify-humidify\"><span style=\"text-decoration:underline\"> Blueair 2-in-1 Pro</span></a> delivers up to 60 hours of moisture from a 1-gallon tank in auto mode, which for most households means one refill every two to three days in a standard bedroom. (Runtime is based on 68 degrees F, 50% relative humidity in auto mode for a 500 sq ft room. Actual runtime varies with conditions.)</p><h4><strong>Step 6: Prioritize a Built-In Humidity Sensor</strong></h4><p>The most useful feature in any humidifier is a built-in humidity sensor with auto shut-off. Without one, guessing how much moisture is in the air risks over-humidifying the space.</p><p>The<a href=\"https://www.epa.gov/mold/brief-guide-mold-moisture-and-your-home\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">EPA recommends keeping indoor humidity between 30% and 50%</span></a> to balance comfort with health. Going above 50% creates conditions that encourage dust mites and mold growth, which is the opposite of what anyone managing<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/blogs/news/air-purifiers-dust-allergies\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">allergies</span></a> or asthma wants. A built-in sensor keeps the room in the right range automatically. More on finding the right<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/blogs/news/humidity-and-health-finding-the-right-balance-at-home\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">humidity balance for health</span></a> is worth reading for anyone dealing with respiratory sensitivities.</p><h4><strong>Step 7: Factor in Maintenance Before Buying</strong></h4><p>Every humidifier needs regular cleaning. Ultrasonic models need the tank cleaned every few days to prevent bacterial growth. Evaporative models need the wick replaced every 1 to 3 months. Warm mist units need regular descaling.</p><p>Before buying, check how easy the tank is to access and clean. Wide openings are significantly easier to maintain than narrow ones. Also, check the filter or wick replacement cost and availability, as those ongoing expenses add up over time.</p><h3><strong>Quick Size Reference</strong></h3><p><strong>Room Type</strong></p><p><strong>Square Footage</strong></p><p><strong>Humidifier Capacity to Look For</strong></p><p><strong>Bedroom</strong></p><p>Under 300 sq ft</p><p>200 to 300 sq ft rated</p><p><strong>Medium room or nursery</strong></p><p>300 to 500 sq ft</p><p>350 to 500 sq ft rated</p><p><strong>Large living area</strong></p><p>500 to 1,000 sq ft</p><p>600 to 1,000 sq ft rated</p><p><strong>Open-plan or multiple rooms</strong></p><p>1,000+ sq ft</p><p>Whole-home or multiple units</p><h3><strong>Placement Matters Too</strong></h3>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
