        "text_EWGj8R": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>Even the right-sized unit will not perform well if placed in a corner facing a wall. Position the humidifier on an elevated surface near the center of the room for the best moisture distribution. Keep the unit at least a few feet from walls, furniture, and electronics. Avoid pointing the mist directly at a bed or pillows, as concentrated moisture on fabric can encourage mold.</p><p>Getting the right size is the most important decision. Placement is the second. Getting both right delivers a noticeable difference in comfort within a few nights. For help deciding what fits a specific space, the Blueair<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/pages/guided-selling\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">guided selling tool</span></a> walks through the key questions in about a minute.</p><h3><strong>FAQs</strong></h3><h4><strong>What size humidifier do I need for a 12x12 bedroom? </strong></h4><p>A 144 sq ft room needs a small personal or tabletop humidifier with a 0.5-1 gallon tank. Any unit rated for 200-300 sq ft will work comfortably in that space.</p><h4><strong>Is it better to get a bigger humidifier than you need? </strong></h4><p>Not necessarily. An oversized humidifier can push humidity above 60%, encouraging mold and dust mites. A unit matched to room size with an auto-shutoff at the right humidity level is the better choice.</p><h4><strong>Can one humidifier work for a whole house? </strong></h4><p>A single portable humidifier typically covers one room effectively. For whole-house coverage, a console humidifier or furnace-integrated unit works better than running multiple portables.</p><h4><strong>How do I know if my humidifier is powerful enough? </strong></h4><p>Check your indoor humidity with a hygrometer. If it stays below 35-40% after running the humidifier for several hours, your unit is likely undersized for the space.</p><h4><strong>Should I get a warm mist or cool mist humidifier for a large room? </strong></h4><p>For large rooms, cool mist evaporative humidifiers tend to distribute moisture more evenly. Warm mist units are quieter but better suited to smaller spaces.</p><h4><strong>How long does a 1-gallon humidifier tank last? </strong></h4><p>A 1-gallon tank on low to auto settings typically lasts 24-60 hours, depending on how dry the air is and how large the space is. Drier climates and larger rooms drain tanks faster.</p>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        }
      },
